Here's the full migration analysis and replacement code.

## Pattern Classification

This is a **timeout enforcement** pattern — a thread is aborted when it exceeds a time limit. It also contains **ThreadAbortException as control flow** (`catch` + `ResetAbort`).

## What Breaks on .NET 8

| API | .NET 8 Behavior |
|-----|-----------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never triggered (dead code) |

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        // CancellationTokenSource is IDisposable — always wrap in using.
        // CancelAfter starts an internal timer that fires Cancel() after timeout.
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            // Task.Run schedules work on the thread pool instead of
            // manually creating and managing a Thread.
            Task<T> task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when
            // the token fires — this is the .NET 6+ replacement for
            // Thread.Join(timeout) + Thread.Abort().
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Cancellation is cooperative, not preemptive** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, the work delegate keeps running after timeout. If `work` does CPU-bound or blocking I/O that never checks a token, it won't stop. Pass `CancellationToken` through to `work` if you need it to actually halt. |
| **Leaking `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using`/dispose it. The `CancelAfter` timer leaks otherwise. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | The `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` guard ensures we only catch our own timeout, not an unrelated cancellation from an outer scope. |
| **`Task.Run` + `.GetAwaiter().GetResult()` can deadlock** | Safe here (no `SynchronizationContext` in console/library code). In UI or ASP.NET classic contexts, prefer an `async` signature returning `Task<T>`. |

## If `work` Must Actually Stop

The fundamental paradigm shift: the work must **cooperate**. Refactor the signature to accept a token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(token), token)
                    .WaitAsync(token)
                    .GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

Callers then check the token at regular checkpoints:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return results;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

If the work is **truly uncooperative** (third-party code, native call), the only reliable option is to move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` — no in-process mechanism in modern .NET can forcibly terminate a thread.
